    <body>
      <Value><![CDATA[## Why I Started Looking at This

When I first built this site, I was so focused on getting the structure right—the document types, the templates, the CSS—that I didn't stop to think about whether everyone could actually *use* it. Then I ran an accessibility check and realized I'd made some choices that quietly locked people out. That's when it clicked: accessibility isn't a feature you add at the end. It's baked into whether your site works.

## The Color Contrast Revelation

I'd picked a color palette that looked beautiful on my screen: soft grays, muted blues, nice contrast *to me*. But when I started checking WCAG standards, I discovered my subtitle text was sitting at a 3.5:1 contrast ratio against the background. Close! But not quite there for normal text. I bumped the font weight, shifted the colors slightly, and suddenly the whole site became more readable. What surprised me: it looked *better*. The stronger contrast wasn't a compromise; it was an improvement everyone could see.

## Focus Rings Changed My Perspective

I'll be honest—I initially hid the default focus ring on buttons because I thought it looked "ugly." Then I tried navigating the site with only my keyboard. Suddenly I was lost, clicking buttons and having no idea where I was. Adding a visible, styled focus ring back in wasn't just the right thing to do; it made the keyboard experience genuinely usable. I even added a bit of color and a subtle glow effect, which made it feel intentional rather than accidental. Now keyboard users can actually see where they are, and it looks good doing it.

## The Compound Effect of Small Fixes

Once I started looking, I found a bunch of little things:
- Link text that was too generic ("click here")
- Images without alt text
- Form inputs that weren't properly labeled
- Color-only indicators (red text alone to mean "error")

None of these were catastrophic on their own, but together they created friction. Fixing them meant writing better link copy, adding alt text that actually described the images, and using icons *plus* text instead of relying on color alone. The work added up, but the payoff was a site that worked for more people, including folks using screen readers, with color blindness, or on slow connections.

## What Stuck With Me

The thing I didn't expect: accessibility improvements made the site better for *everyone*. Better contrast helps people on mobile devices in bright sunlight. Keyboard navigation is faster for power users. Clear link text helps people with dyslexia and makes search engines happy. It turns out that when you build for the edges, you often build something stronger at the center.

I'm not done learning about accessibility—there's always more—but this first pass taught me that it's not an afterthought or a burden. It's part of the job.]]></Value>
    </body>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[How I discovered that color contrast, keyboard navigation, and proper labels aren't just nice—they're essential—and how building for accessibility made the entire site better.]]></Value>
